# 2026-05-18 (slot bravo) — truly-ephemeral crashed-write / cache artifacts.
# Audited the 9967 untracked entries: the patterns below are 100% disk cruft
# (atomic-write fragments from killed writers, ISO/stamp markers, ephemeral
# pillar diffs). Total ~85 files. The other 9882 untracked are legitimate
# uncommitted work (milestone envelopes, memories, specs) that needs
# per-owning-slot commits — NOT a .gitignore problem.
#
# Atomic-write temp fragments — every JSON writer in PRISM uses
# `writeFileSync(path + '.tmp.' + pid, json); renameSync(...)`. A crashed
# writer leaves the .tmp.<pid> orphan forever (the real file is either the
# previous good version or the next successful write — the .tmp is never
# semantically meaningful). Observed: 75 such fragments this session.
*.tmp.[0-9]*

# Ephemeral pillar-diff patches written by mcp-server/data/state/.dd-pillar*
# generators. Get superseded by the next run; carrying them in git is noise.
.dd-pillar*.patch
mcp-server/data/state/.dd-pillar*.patch

# Timestamp markers (e.g., .wiring-audit-suggest-last.iso) — one ISO string
# tracking last-run time. Regenerable; carries no signal.
*-last.iso
mcp-server/data/state/*-last.iso

# Generic stamp files used as throttle markers by hooks/crons.
*.stamp
*-stamp
